3. Trie

a.
We're going to need some way to store and efficiently identify valid English words. A great way to
do this is with the Trie data structure, which we're going to implement in this section.

We've gone ahead and written Trie.mli - take a look at the interface.

The first decision to make is what you want your type `t` to look like. Feel free to talk
to your TA about your ideas before diving in.

Hint: we suggest using mutable datatypes

One you're happy with your `t`, go ahead and fill in the `create` function.


b.
Let's fill in `Trie.insert`. This function adds an input valid word into our `Trie`. (We'll use
this later on to insert a bunch of real words into our `Trie`.)

c.
Let's fill in `Trie.get_longest_valid_prefix`. This function should return the
longest prefix of the input that is a valid English word, according to our `Trie`.

Let's look at an example:

```
input: ADOGS
output: ADO
```

If this example doesn't sense to you, grab a TA to talk it through.

There are a few tests for the implementation of `Trie.get_longest_valid_prefix` in
`tests/spelling_extension`. When you're done with your implementation, make sure to run
the tests in that directory to check your implementation:
    
`$ dune runtest tests/spelling_extension`

4. Putting it all together

a.
Let's fill in the `Game.trie` variable, where we currently create a set of valid English
words and then...ignore it. Instead, let's insert all those words into our
`Trie`. Remember to remove the `ignore trie` statement in the same file.

Hint: Make sure that case doesn't matter when we're matching an eaten string against
valid words

b.
Let's update `Game.maybe_consume_apple` to detect the longest valid English substring
inside the snake, and make any necessary updates to the `Game` if we find a word. We will
consider the necessary updates to the `Snake` below.

Most of the work here will be in valid word detection. Feel free to talk to your TA about
how you plan to implement this before diving in.

We recommend adding a rule that words must be at least 3 characters in length.

c.
Finally, let's handle necessary updates to the `Snake` when we spell a valid word.

Let's fill in `Snake.clear_spelled_word`. It has this signature:

```val clear_spelled_word : t -> spelled_word:string -> unit```

Don't forget to actually call this function in the `Game`.

You should now have a full working version of the game.

d. Let's add in one more nice tweak to actually display the words the player has spelled.
Create a new field in `Game` that stores all words the player has successfully spelled so
far and a function to expose them.

We've already added this function to `Game.mli`:

`val spelled_words : t -> string list`

Once you've implemented this field, try running the game - we've already added logic to
